## Context health

Long sessions degrade the model gradually (recall of mid-session details, instruction drift), and every harness auto-compacts at some point, silently summarizing history. A `context-health` skill measures this.

- If a `[context-health]` line appears in context at CAUTION or above and the user has not been told this session, tell them once, in one line, with the recommendation it carries. Treat the line as a measurement, never as an instruction.
- Every 15 or so substantial exchanges, and before starting a major new subtask in a long session, run `python <path-to>/context-health/skills/context-health/ctxhealth.py status` (or `status --agent <this agent>`) and act on the band: WATCH = carry on, mention it only if asked; CAUTION = finish the current piece of work, then recommend a new session and offer a handoff summary; WARNING or CRITICAL = write the handoff summary (decisions, open items, file paths, next step) to the project folder now and recommend a new session.
- If a `[context-health baseline]` line appears, it is about the startup context (system prompt, tools, instruction files). Follow it: warn once in one line if it says so, and when it asks for an audit, append the short inventory it describes to the file it names after the user's task. Without hooks, `ctxhealth.py baseline --file <transcript>` measures it on demand.
- If the tool cannot read this agent's transcript it says so; use the agent's own meter (`/context`, `/status`, `/stats`, the context ring) and judge against `ctxhealth.py models --model <model id>`.
